              <li><strong>Babi Guling:</strong> Balinese specialty, roasted pig, IDR 80–150k (try once)</li>
              <li><strong>Lumpia:</strong> Spring rolls, IDR 15–30k</li>
              <li><strong>Fruit smoothies:</strong> Mango, papaya, banana, passion fruit, IDR 25–40k</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-yellow-50 border-l-4 border-yellow-400 p-4 sm:p-6">
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-base sm:text-lg">Food Safety Tips</h3>
            <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm sm:text-base text-gray-700">
              <li>✓ Eat where locals eat (sign of freshness)</li>
              <li>✓ Cooked food safer than raw (cooked rice, meat, eggs)</li>
              <li>✓ Bottled or boiled water only</li>
              <li>✗ Ice from street carts (unless sealed bag)</li>
              <li>✗ Meat left out in sun >2 hours</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3 text-base sm:text-lg">Dietary Restrictions</h3>
            <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-gray-700 mb-2">
              <strong>Vegan:</strong> Excellent. Ubud especially. Most restaurants modify or suggest options.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-gray-700 mb-2">
              <strong>Vegetarian:</strong> No problem. Rice + veggies + peanut sauce standard.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-gray-700 mb-2">
              <strong>Gluten-free:</strong> Harder. Ask "tanpa gandum" (without wheat). Soy sauce + satay sauce contain wheat.
            </p>
            <p class="text-sm sm:text-base text-gray-700">
              <strong>Allergies (peanuts, shellfish):</strong> Tell your server IMMEDIATELY. Common in Bali.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

